    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 495</span><span class="rf-text">Born, most likely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the daughter of Acacius, a bear keeper for the Green faction</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">524</span><span class="rf-text">After the death of Empress Euphemia, Justin issues an edict allowing actresses to marry senators, opening the way to her marriage with Justinian</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">527</span><span class="rf-text">Crowned augusta when Justinian succeeds to the throne</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">528</span><span class="rf-text">With Justinian orders the closing of the brothels and the arrest of their keepers</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">532</span><span class="rf-text">During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nika-riots">Nika riots</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>'s account, speaks against flight in the emperor's council</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">541</span><span class="rf-text">With Antonina, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>, brings about the fall of the praetorian prefect John the Cappadocian</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">547</span><span class="rf-text">The mosaics of San Vitale in Ravenna, showing her in full imperial dress, are completed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">548</span><span class="rf-text">Dies and is buried in the Church of the Holy Apostles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Much of Theodora's early life is uncertain, including the date and place of her birth; the modern consensus is that she was born around 495 and raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, though later sources placed her birth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, Paphlagonia or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. Her father, Acacius, was a bear keeper for the Green faction of the Hippodrome, and her mother, whose name was not recorded, was a dancer and actress. She had an elder sister, Comito, and a younger, Anastasia. After her father died, the Greens gave her stepfather's post to someone else for a bribe; her mother brought the children before the crowd as suppliants, and when the Greens refused them the Blue faction gave the family the position.</p><p>The foremost source for her youth is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>'s Secret History, which some modern scholars regard as often unreliable and slanderous. According to it she worked as an actress before adolescence, and acting then included indecent shows on stage and sexual services off it; John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> also says she came "from the brothel". David Potter judges that beneath Procopius's inventions "there lies the semblance of fact". She later went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> as the concubine of Hecebolus, governor of the Libyan Pentapolis, and after they parted travelled to Alexandria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. Some historians speculate that she became a Miaphysite in Alexandria, but there is no reliable evidence of it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Theodora returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and met Justinian, likely sometime before 524. He wished to marry her, but a law from the time of Constantine barred men of senatorial rank from marrying actresses, and Justin's wife, Empress Euphemia, strongly opposed the match. After Euphemia died in 524, Justin issued an edict that same year allowing actresses to marry senators, and the couple married. The same law excused the children of former actresses, so Theodora's illegitimate daughter could marry a relative of the late emperor Anastasius; a great-granddaughter through this line married Peter, brother of the emperor Maurice. A story that she made a son named Ioannes disappear is doubted by James Allan Evans, since she openly acknowledged her daughter.</p><p>When Justinian succeeded to the throne in 527, Theodora was crowned augusta. According to the Secret History she took part in state councils and had great influence over her husband. In Novel 8.1 of 535, a law against corruption under which provincial officials swore an oath to the emperor and to Theodora, Justinian called her his "partner in my deliberations". <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, confirmed by the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corippus">Corippus</a>, says the couple changed the court ceremonies so that officials kissed the feet of both, and required to be called "master" and "mistress".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In January 532 the Blue and Green factions, united against the emperor, rioted under the cry "Nika", burned public buildings and proclaimed Hypatius, nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasius-i-dicorus">Anastasius I</a>, emperor. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, as Justinian and his officials prepared to flee, Theodora spoke in the council against flight, saying that for one who has reigned it is intolerable to be a fugitive and that "royal purple" is the noblest shroud. Justinian's troops attacked the crowd in the Hippodrome, killing over 30,000, and Hypatius and his brother Pompeius were put to death, in one source at her insistence. J. A. S. Evans notes that the speech echoes advice once given to the tyrant Dionysius of Syracuse and suggests Procopius dramatised the scene.</p><p>Theodora and Justinian commissioned many buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the most famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, and she was involved in the Church of Sergius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Bacchus</a>, whose inscription praises her care for the destitute. In 528 the couple ordered the brothels closed and their keepers and procurers arrested; she paid the owners the purchase price of the women, freed them and gave each clothing and a gold nomisma. She founded the Metanoia convent on the Asian side of the Dardanelles, where former prostitutes could support themselves, and she compelled the general Artabanes to take back the wife he had abandoned. She also arranged marriages that bound her family to the old nobility, among them her niece Sophia's to Justinian's nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Theodora was reputed to be a Miaphysite and worked against her husband's support of the Chalcedonian church, and pro-Chalcedonians accused her of fostering heresy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> and Evagrius Scholasticus, followed by some modern historians, suggested the couple only pretended to disagree. She sheltered Miaphysite leaders such as Severus and Anthimus, whom she had helped to become Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and hid in her quarters for ten years after his excommunication. When Pope Silverius refused to lift the anathema on Anthimus, she had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> find a pretext to remove him; Silverius was exiled to a rocky island, where he starved to death, and Vigilius took his place. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobades">Nobatae</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, she had the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a> delay her husband's Chalcedonian missionaries so that hers arrived first.</p><p>Sources generally describe her as vindictive but determined and loyal, and also as cruel and inspiring fear. Procopius alleges that she ordered the murder of the Ostrogothic queen Amalasuintha, which a surviving letter from King Theodahad independently supports. His Secret History, written after her death, portrays her as shameless, cruel and lustful: it claims she "rounded up" 500 prostitutes and confined them in the convent, where some leapt the walls to escape, whereas John Malalas wrote that she freed the girls from the yoke of their wretched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Procopius also accused her of unfairly championing wives charged with adultery.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Theodora remained active at court in her last years. She was at odds with the praetorian prefect John the Cappadocian, a close ally of the emperor whom she resented for his influence, his wealth and his slanders of her. In 541 she and Antonina, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>, laid a trap: Antonina led John's daughter to believe in a plot to put Belisarius on the throne, John was caught making treasonous remarks, and he was removed. She was also hostile to Justinian's cousin Germanus, while the prefect Peter Barsymes was her ally. According to the Secret History she pressed for her grandson Anastasius to marry Joannina, the daughter and heiress of Belisarius and Antonina, against the parents' will, and the couple eventually married.</p><p>The mosaics of the Basilica of San Vitale in Ravenna, completed in 547, show her in full imperial dress with a communion chalice. She died on 28 June 548. Victor of Tunnuna records the death in Greek terms usually translated as "cancer", probably meaning a suppurating ulcer or malignant tumour; later accounts call it breast cancer, which the original report does not say. She was buried in the Church of the Holy Apostles, and in a procession in 559 Justinian visited her tomb and lit candles in her memory. Her niece Sophia later became empress beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Theodora is recognised as a saint in the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-orthodox-churches">Oriental Orthodox Churches</a> and commemorated on 14 November. The Miaphysites, who regard her as one of their own, believed her influence over Justinian was so strong that after her death he worked for harmony between them and the Chalcedonians and kept his promise to protect her community of Miaphysite refugees in the Hormisdas Palace; she had given much political support to the ministry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-baradaeus">Jacob Baradaeus</a>. Olbia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, now Qasr Libya, was renamed Theodorias after her, and Cululis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> Proconsularis Theodoriana. According to one historian, "No empress left so profound a mark on the imagination of her people as did Theodora." Plays, operas, films and novels have been written about her.</p><p>Her reputation rests largely on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, who praised her courage in the Wars, slandered her in the Secret History, published only in 1623, and flattered her piety in the Buildings. James Allan Evans holds that the hostile portrait shows how the upper classes felt threatened by her power, Averil Cameron that its demons were a contemporary way to rationalise the inexplicable, and Peter Heather calls it arguably the most effective character assassination ever written. Anthony Kaldellis and Clive Foss answer that independent sources confirm her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> background, the marriage law and her ruthlessness, and Foss thinks Procopius embellished rather than invented.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/hymn-of-kassiani">Hymn of Kassiani<span>c. 843</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Theodora rose from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> and rule beside him as empress from 527 to 548. She advised him, closed brothels, sheltered Miaphysites against his church policy and removed rivals by intrigue, and nearly everything told of her passes through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, whose praise and slander historians still weigh against other sources.</em></p>

